DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ffssccoonnffiigg()  system  call  is part of the suite of file‐descriptor‐
       based mount facilities in Linux.

       ffssccoonnffiigg() is used to supply parameters to and issue  commands  against
       the  filesystem configuration context associated with the file descrip‐
       tor  _f_d.   Filesystem  configuration  contexts  can  be  created   with
       ffssooppeenn(2)  or  be  instantiated from an extant filesystem instance with
       ffssppiicckk(2).

       The _c_m_d argument indicates the command to  be  issued.   Some  commands
       supply parameters to the context (equivalent to mount options specified
       with mmoouunntt(8)), while others are meta‐operations on the filesystem con‐
       text.  The list of valid _c_m_d values are:

              FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__FFLLAAGG
                     Set the flag parameter named by _k_e_y.  _v_a_l_u_e must be NULL,
                     and _a_u_x must be 0.

              FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__SSTTRRIINNGG
                     Set the string parameter named by _k_e_y to the value speci‐
                     fied by _v_a_l_u_e.  _v_a_l_u_e points to a null‐terminated string,
                     and _a_u_x must be 0.

              FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__BBIINNAARRYY
                     Set  the  blob  parameter named by _k_e_y to the contents of
                     the binary blob specified by _v_a_l_u_e.  _v_a_l_u_e points to  the
                     start of a buffer that is _a_u_x bytes in length.

              FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__FFDD
                     Set  the file parameter named by _k_e_y to the open file de‐
                     scription referenced by the file descriptor  _a_u_x.   _v_a_l_u_e
                     must be NULL.

                     You may also use FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__SSTTRRIINNGG for file parameters,
                     with  _v_a_l_u_e  set to a null‐terminated string containing a
                     base‐10 representation of  the  file  descriptor  number.
                     This  mechanism  is  primarily intended for compatibility
                     with older mmoouunntt(2)‐based programs, and  only  works  for
                     parameters that _o_n_l_y accept file descriptor arguments.

              FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__PPAATTHH
                     Set  the  path  parameter named by _k_e_y to the object at a
                     provided path, resolved in a similar manner to ooppeennaatt(2).
                     _v_a_l_u_e points to a null‐terminated  pathname  string,  and
                     _a_u_x  is  equivalent  to  the _d_i_r_f_d argument to ooppeennaatt(2).
                     See  ooppeennaatt(2)  for  an  explanation  of  the  need   for
                     FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__PPAATTHH.

                     You may also use FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__SSTTRRIINNGG for path parameters,
                     the behaviour of which is equivalent to FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__PPAATTHH
                     with _a_u_x set to AATT__FFDDCCWWDD.

              FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__PPAATTHH__EEMMPPTTYY
                     As  with  FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__PPAATTHH,  except  that if _v_a_l_u_e is an
                     empty string, the file descriptor specified by _a_u_x is op‐
                     erated on directly and may be any type of file (not  just
                     a  directory).   This  is  equivalent to the behaviour of
                     AATT__EEMMPPTTYY__PPAATTHH with most "*at()" system calls.  If _a_u_x  is
                     AATT__FFDDCCWWDD,  the parameter will be set to the current work‐
                     ing directory of the calling process.

              FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE
                     This command instructs the filesystem driver to instanti‐
                     ate an instance of the filesystem in the kernel with  the
                     parameters specified in the filesystem configuration con‐
                     text.  _k_e_y and _v_a_l_u_e must be NULL, and _a_u_x must be 0.

                     This command can only be issued once in the lifetime of a
                     filesystem  context.   If  the  operation  succeeds,  the
                     filesystem context associated with file descriptor _f_d now
                     references the created filesystem instance, and is placed
                     into a special "awaiting‐mount" mode that allows  you  to
                     use ffssmmoouunntt(2) to create a mount object from the filesys‐
                     tem  instance.  If the operation fails, in most cases the
                     filesystem context is placed in a failed mode and  cannot
                     be used for any further ffssccoonnffiigg() operations (though you
                     may  still  retrieve diagnostic messages through the mes‐
                     sage retrieval interface, as described in the correspond‐
                     ing subsection of ffssooppeenn(2)).

                     This command can only be issued against  filesystem  con‐
                     figuration contexts that were created with ffssooppeenn(2).  In
                     order  to  create  a  filesystem  instance,  the  calling
                     process must have the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability.

                     An important thing to be aware of is that the Linux  ker‐
                     nel  will  _s_i_l_e_n_t_l_y reuse extant filesystem instances de‐
                     pending on the filesystem type and the configured parame‐
                     ters (each filesystem driver has its own policy  for  how
                     filesystem  instances  are  reused).  This means that the
                     filesystem instance "created" by FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE may,
                     in fact, be a reference to an extant filesystem  instance
                     in  the  kernel.  (For reference, this behaviour also ap‐
                     plies to mmoouunntt(2).)

                     One side‐effect of this behaviour is that  if  an  extant
                     filesystem  instance is reused, _a_l_l parameters configured
                     for this filesystem configuration  context  are  _s_i_l_e_n_t_l_y
                     _i_g_n_o_r_e_d (with the exception of the _r_o and _r_w flag parame‐
                     ters;  if  the  state of the read‐only flag in the extant
                     filesystem instance and the filesystem configuration con‐
                     text do not match, this  operation  will  return  EEBBUUSSYY).
                     This  also  means  that FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__RREECCOONNFFIIGGUURREE commands
                     issued against the  "created"  filesystem  instance  will
                     also  affect any mount objects associated with the extant
                     filesystem instance.

                     Programs that need to  ensure  that  they  create  a  new
                     filesystem  instance  with  specific parameters (notably,
                     security‐related parameters such as _a_c_l to  enable  POSIX
                     ACLs—as     described     in     aaccll(5))    should    use
                     FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE__EEXXCCLL instead.

              FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE__EEXXCCLL (since Linux 6.6)
                     As with FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE, except that  the  kernel  is
                     instructed  to not reuse extant filesystem instances.  If
                     the operation would be forced to reuse an extant filesys‐
                     tem instance, this operation will return EEBBUUSSYY instead.

                     As a result (unlike FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE), if this  opera‐
                     tion  succeeds  then the calling process can be sure that
                     all of the parameters successfully configured with ffssccoonn‐‐
                     ffiigg() will actually be applied to the created  filesystem
                     instance.

              FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__RREECCOONNFFIIGGUURREE
                     This command instructs the filesystem driver to apply the
                     parameters specified in the filesystem configuration con‐
                     text  to the extant filesystem instance referenced by the
                     filesystem configuration context.  _k_e_y and _v_a_l_u_e must  be
                     NULL, and _a_u_x must be 0.

                     This  is  primarily  intended for use with ffssppiicckk(2), but
                     may also be used to modify the parameters of a filesystem
                     instance after FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE was used to create  it
                     and  a mount object was created using ffssmmoouunntt(2).  In or‐
                     der to reconfigure an  extant  filesystem  instance,  the
                     calling process must have the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability.

                     If  the operation succeeds, the filesystem context is re‐
                     set but remains in reconfiguration mode and thus  can  be
                     reused  for subsequent FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__RREECCOONNFFIIGGUURREE commands.
                     If the operation fails, in most cases the filesystem con‐
                     text is placed in a failed mode and cannot  be  used  for
                     any  further  ffssccoonnffiigg() operations (though you may still
                     retrieve diagnostic  messages  through  the  message  re‐
                     trieval interface, as described in the corresponding sub‐
                     section of ffssooppeenn(2)).

       Parameters  specified  with  FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT___*  do not take effect until a
       corresponding FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__CCRREEAATTEE or  FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__CCMMDD__RREECCOONNFFIIGGUURREE  command
       is issued.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On  success, ffssccoonnffiigg() returns 0.  On error, -1 is returned, and _e_r_r_n_o
       is set to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
       If an error occurs, the filesystem driver may provide additional infor‐
       mation about the error through  the  message  retrieval  interface  for
       filesystem  configuration contexts.  This additional information can be
       retrieved at any time by calling rreeaadd(2) on the filesystem instance  or
       filesystem  configuration context referenced by the file descriptor _f_d.
       (See the "Message retrieval interface" subsection in ffssooppeenn(2) for more
       details on the message format.)

       Even after an error occurs, the filesystem configuration context is _n_o_t
       invalidated, and thus can still be used with other ffssccoonnffiigg() commands.
       This means that users can probe support for filesystem parameters on  a
       per‐parameter basis, and adjust which parameters they wish to set.

       The  error  values  given below result from filesystem type independent
       errors.  Each filesystem type may have its own special errors  and  its
       own special behavior.  See the Linux kernel source code for details.

