       Each filesystem driver is responsible for parsing most parameters spec‐
       ified  with  ffssccoonnffiigg(),  meaning  that individual filesystems may have
       very different behaviour when encountering  parameters  with  the  same
       name.   In  general, you should not assume that the behaviour of ffssccoonn‐‐
       ffiigg() when specifying a parameter to one filesystem type will match the
       behaviour of the same parameter with a different filesystem type.

       However, the following generic parameters apply to all filesystems  and
       have  unified  behaviour.  They are set using the listed FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT___*
       command.

       rroo and rrww (FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__FFLLAAGG)
              Configure whether the filesystem instance is read‐only.

       ddiirrssyynncc (FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__FFLLAAGG)
              Make directory changes on this filesystem instance synchronous.

       ssyynncc and aassyynncc (FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__FFLLAAGG)
              Configure whether writes on this  filesystem  instance  will  be
              made synchronous (as though the OO__SSYYNNCC flag to ooppeenn(2) was spec‐
              ified for all file opens in this filesystem instance).

       llaazzyyttiimmee and nnoollaazzyyttiimmee (FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__FFLLAAGG)
              Configure  whether to reduce on‐disk updates of inode timestamps
              on this filesystem instance (as  described  in  the  MMSS__LLAAZZYYTTIIMMEE
              section of mmoouunntt(2)).

       mmaanndd and nnoommaanndd (FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__FFLLAAGG)
              Configure  whether  the filesystem instance should permit manda‐
              tory locking.  Since Linux 5.15, mandatory locking has been dep‐
              recated and setting this flag is a no‐op.

       ssoouurrccee (FFSSCCOONNFFIIGG__SSEETT__SSTTRRIINNGG)
              This parameter is equivalent to the _s_o_u_r_c_e parameter  passed  to
              mmoouunntt(2)  for the same filesystem type, and is usually the path‐
              name of a block device containing the filesystem.  This  parame‐
              ter  may  only  be set once per filesystem configuration context
              transaction.

       In addition, any filesystem parameters associated with  Linux  Security
       Modules (LSMs) are also generic with respect to the underlying filesys‐
       tem.   See the documentation for the LSM you wish to configure for more
       details.

